WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:04.705
My father's family name was Pirrip, and my own first name was Philip. But all my

2
00:00:04.706 --> 00:00:11.205
young tongue could say of these names was "Pip." So that is what I called myself—Pip.

3
00:00:11.206 --> 00:00:16.943
I never saw my father or mother or any pictures of them, for they lived before

4
00:00:16.944 --> 00:00:20.610
the days of photographs. I lived with my sister, who was more than twenty years older

5
00:00:20.611 --> 00:00:26.039
than I. My first vivid impression of things was gained one memorable late afternoon in the

6
00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:31.452
churchyard. At that time I understood that my father and mother were dead, as were my

7
00:00:31.453 --> 00:00:37.277
five little brothers. Alone in this bleak place overgrown with nettles, I shivered with fear and

8
00:00:37.278 --> 00:00:44.920
began to cry. "Stop your noise!" cried a terrible voice as a man rose up from

9
00:00:44.921 --> 00:00:49.777
among the graves. "Keep still or I'll cut your throat!" He was a frightening man dressed

10
00:00:49.778 --> 00:00:54.507
all in gray with an iron on his leg like prisoners wore. "Oh! Don't cut my

11
00:00:54.508 --> 00:00:58.253
throat, sir," I pleaded in terror. "Tell me your name! Quick!" "Pip, sir." "Show me where

12
00:00:58.254 --> 00:01:01.833
you live. Point out the place." We lived in the marsh country down by the river,

13
00:01:01.834 --> 00:01:06.063
twenty miles from the sea. I pointed to the flat lands, where our village lay a

14
00:01:06.064 --> 00:01:10.738
mile or more from the church. After looking at me for a moment, the man turned

15
00:01:10.739 --> 00:01:15.904
me upside down and emptied my pockets. There was nothing in them but a piece of

16
00:01:15.905 --> 00:01:18.491
bread. When I was right-side up again, I was seated on a high tombstone, trembling, while

17
00:01:18.492 --> 00:01:24.745
he ate the bread ravenously. "Where's your mother?" asked the man. "There, sir!" I pointed to

18
00:01:24.746 --> 00:01:30.729
her tombstone. "And is that your father alongside her?" "Yes, sir." "Who do you live with—supposing

19
00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:33.388
you're allowed to live, which I haven't made up my mind about?" "My sister, sir. Mrs. Joe

20
00:01:33.389 --> 00:01:34.816
Gargery, wife of Joe Gargery the blacksmith." "Blacksmith, eh?" And he looked down at his leg.

21
00:01:34.817 --> 00:01:40.459
"Do you know what a file is?" "Yes, sir," I said as the man tilted me

22
00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:45.800
back as far as he could to frighten me. "You get me a file." He tilted

23
00:01:45.801 --> 00:01:51.627
me again. "And you get me food." He tilted me yet again. "You bring them both

24
00:01:51.628 --> 00:01:56.768
to me, or I'll rip your heart and liver out." I was so frightened that I

25
00:01:56.769 --> 00:02:08.463
clung to him with both hands. "If you would kindly please keep me upright, sir, perhaps

26
00:02:08.464 --> 00:02:12.114
I wouldn't be sick and perhaps I could pay more attention." He gave me a tremendous

27
00:02:12.115 --> 00:02:18.677
dip and roll. "You bring me all that tomorrow morning. You do that and don't say

28
00:02:18.678 --> 00:02:23.915
a word to anyone that you've seen a person such as me, and you shall be

29
00:02:23.916 --> 00:02:30.756
allowed to live. You fail and your heart and liver shall be torn out and eaten."

30
00:02:30.757 --> 00:02:35.090
"Now I'm not alone. There's a young man who hid with me, and in comparison with

31
00:02:35.091 --> 00:02:41.582
him, I am an angel. It is with great difficulty that I am keeping that young

32
00:02:41.583 --> 00:02:47.740
man from harming you. Now what do you say?" I said I would get him the

33
00:02:47.741 --> 00:02:54.415
file and what bits of food I could. "Good . . . good night, sir," I

34
00:02:54.416 --> 00:02:58.565
faltered. I watched as he limped away toward the low wall around the church. When I

35
00:02:58.566 --> 00:03:02.994
saw him turning to look at me, I ran as fast as I could toward home.

36
00:03:02.995 --> 00:03:12.732
But soon I looked over my shoulder. The marshes were just a long, black, horizontal line

37
00:03:12.733 --> 00:03:19.145
then. On the edge of the river, I could make out two things. One of these

38
00:03:19.146 --> 00:03:23.724
was the beacon by which the sailors steered, the other a gallows that had once held

39
00:03:23.725 --> 00:03:27.970
a pirate. The convict was limping toward the gallows as if he were the pirate come

40
00:03:27.971 --> 00:03:32.375
to life. Now I was frightened again and ran home without stopping. Joe's forge, where he

41
00:03:32.376 --> 00:03:37.216
did his blacksmith work, was attached to our house. When I arrived home, the forge was

42
00:03:37.217 --> 00:03:42.049
shut up, and Joe was sitting alone in the kitchen. "Mrs. Joe has been out a

43
00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:47.121
dozen times looking for you, Pip. And she's out now." "Is she?" "Yes, Pip, and what's

44
00:03:47.122 --> 00:03:50.621
worse, she's got Tickler with her." Tickler was a piece of cane that my sister used

45
00:03:50.622 --> 00:03:54.859
to hit me. "Here she comes!" said Joe. "Hide behind the door." My sister threw open

46
00:03:54.860 --> 00:04:01.359
the door and, finding an obstruction behind it, pulled me out and used Tickler on me.

47
00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:06.423
"Where have you been, you young monkey?" "I've been to the churchyard." "If it weren't for

48
00:04:06.424 --> 00:04:12.764
me, you'd have been to the churchyard long ago and stayed there. Who brought you up?"

49
00:04:12.765 --> 00:04:17.843
"You did." I cried as I sat down on my stool by the fire. "That's right,

50
00:04:17.844 --> 00:04:25.002
and I'd never do it again. It's bad enough to be a blacksmith's wife without being

51
00:04:25.003 --> 00:04:31.653
your mother." She put Tickler away. Then she set to work making our tea and slicing

52
00:04:31.654 --> 00:04:35.161
and buttering bread for Joe and me. Although I was hungry, I dared not eat my

53
00:04:35.162 --> 00:04:42.986
slice. I had to save something for my dreadful acquaintance and his young friend, so I

54
00:04:42.987 --> 00:04:47.812
resolved to put my hunk of bread and butter down the leg of my trousers. Later

55
00:04:47.813 --> 00:04:52.391
I managed to slip away and deposit the food in my bedroom upstairs. It was Christmas Eve,

56
00:04:52.392 --> 00:04:57.066
and for an hour I had to stir the pudding for the next day. Afterward I

57
00:04:57.067 --> 00:05:03.971
sat near the fire, taking a final warming before going to bed. A booming sound came

58
00:05:03.972 --> 00:05:10.296
from over the marshes. "Hark! What is that, Joe?" I asked. "A convict escaped last night,

59
00:05:10.297 --> 00:05:11.883
and they fired a warning. And now it appears they're firing warning of another." "Who's firing?"

60
00:05:11.884 --> 00:05:17.129
"Drat that boy," said my sister, looking up from her needlework and frowning at me. "What

61
00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:23.129
a questioner he is. The firing comes from the prison ships across the marshes." "I wonder

62
00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:27.716
who's put into prison ships and why they're put there?" I said. This was too much

63
00:05:27.717 --> 00:05:31.939
for my sister, who rapped me on the head with her thimble. "I didn't bring you

64
00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:35.613
up to bother everyone with questions. People are put in prison ships because they murder or

65
00:05:35.614 --> 00:05:41.105
rob or do all sorts of bad things. And they always begin by asking questions. Now

66
00:05:41.106 --> 00:05:46.272
go to bed!" I was never allowed a candle to light my way to bed. As

67
00:05:46.273 --> 00:05:49.359
I went upstairs in the dark, I thought how conveniently close the prison ships were for

68
00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:56.922
me because I was clearly on my way there. I had begun by asking questions, and

69
00:05:56.923 --> 00:06:03.153
now, because of the convict, I was going to rob Mrs. Joe. At the first light

70
00:06:03.154 --> 00:06:06.915
of day, I crept downstairs to the pantry. I stole some bread, some cheese, half a

71
00:06:06.916 --> 00:06:15.740
jar of mincemeat, some brandy, a bone with very little meat on it, and a beautiful

72
00:06:15.741 --> 00:06:19.082
pork pie. There was a door in the kitchen that led to Joe's forge. I unlocked

73
00:06:19.083 --> 00:06:25.916
it and got a file from among Joe's tools. Then I ran for the misty marshes.

